LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish is read the way it is written. Learn the few letters that differ from English, where the stress falls, and ¿ ¡.","text":"Spanish spelling is regular: once you know the letters, you can read any word aloud and usually write a word you hear. Compare English though, through and tough. Spanish has five clear vowels, a few letters that differ from English, and simple stress rules. How it works Vowels. There are five, and each has one short, clear sound. They never change into \"uh\" as in English banana. Letter Sounds like Example a a in father casa (house) e e in bed mesa (table) i ee in see, but short sí (yes) o o in go, but short, without the w glide at the end ocho (eight) u oo in food, short uno (one) Letters that differ from English. Letter How to say it Example h silent hola, hotel j, and g before e / i a breathy sound from the throat, like a strong h José, gente ñ ny as in canyon España, mañana ll, y y as in yes (in some regions closer to j in jam; in Argentina and Uruguay like sh) calle, yo r between vowels one quick tap, like tt in American better pero (but) rr, and r at the start of a word or after n, l, s a rolled r perro (dog), rojo, Enrique b, v the same sound vaso, bar c before e / i, z th in think in most of Spain; s in Latin America, the Canary Islands and parts of southern Spain cine, zona c before a / o / u, g before a / o / u k; g in go casa, gracias qu k (the u is silent) queso Stress. Every word has one strong syllable. Three rules find it: A word ending in a vowel, n or s is stressed on the second-to-last syllable: CA-sa, HA-blan, LU-nes. A word ending in any other consonant is stressed on the last syllable: ho-TEL, ciu-DAD. A written accent breaks the rules and shows the stressed syllable: ca-FÉ, te-LÉ-fo-no, Jo-SÉ. Questions and exclamations have a mark at the start, upside down: ¿Cómo te llamas? ¡Hola! When to use it Reading a new word. Say each letter, then put the stress where the rules say. Spelling your name. People will ask ¿Cómo se escribe? (How do you spell it?). The letter names: a, be, ce, de, e, efe, ge, hache, i, jota, ka, ele, eme, ene, eñe, o, pe, cu, erre, ese, te, u, uve (Spain) / ve (Latin America), uve doble (Spain) / doble ve or doble u (Latin America), equis, ye, zeta. Writing. A missing accent or ñ can change the meaning: papa (potato, Latin America) and papá (dad); ano (anus) and año (year). Examples Spanish English hola hello (the h is silent) España Spain la calle the street el perro the dog (rolled rr) pero but (one tap) el teléfono the telephone (te-LÉ-fo-no) ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? Good to know ch sounds as in church: chocolate. Common mistakes ✗ Como te llamas? → ✓ ¿Cómo te llamas? — a question needs ¿ at the start, and question words have an accent. ✗ Espana → ✓ España — n and ñ are two different letters. ✗ pero (for \"dog\") → ✓ perro — one r is a tap; rr is a roll, and it is a different word. ✗ Jose → ✓ José — the name is stressed on the last syllable, and the accent shows it. Practice Where is the stress? Write the stressed syllable. mesa hotel café hablan Write the word correctly. Espana (Spain) el pero (the dog) Como te llamas? (What's your name?) ola (hello) Answers ME-sa (ends in a vowel) ho-TEL (ends in l) ca-FÉ (written accent) HA-blan (ends in n) España el perro ¿Cómo te llamas? hola","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say hello at any time of day, give your name, ask someone's name, react politely and say goodbye.","text":"These are the first sentences you will use, and you can learn them as fixed phrases before you know the grammar inside them. One difference from English matters from day one: Spanish has two ways to say \"you\". Use tú with friends, family, children and people your age; use usted with older people, strangers in formal situations, and customers or senior people at work. Many phrases below come in both versions. Key phrases Saying hello Spanish English Hola. Hi. / Hello. (any time, any person) Buenos días. Good morning. (until noon in Latin America; until lunch, about 2 p.m., in Spain) Buenas tardes. Good afternoon. / Good evening. (until it gets dark) Buenas noches. Good evening. / Good night. (after dark, also to say goodbye) ¿Qué tal? How's it going? (very common, friendly) ¿Cómo estás? / ¿Cómo está usted? How are you? (informal / formal) Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? Fine, thanks. And you? Introducing yourself and others Spanish English Me llamo Ana. My name is Ana. Soy Ana. I'm Ana. ¿Cómo te llamas? What's your name? (informal) ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) Este es Pedro. / Esta es Lucía. This is Pedro. / This is Lucía. Mucho gusto. Nice to meet you. Encantado. / Encantada. Pleased to meet you. (a man says encantado, a woman encantada) Igualmente. Likewise. Saying goodbye Spanish English Adiós. Goodbye. Hasta luego. See you later. (also for \"bye\" in a shop) Hasta mañana. See you tomorrow. Nos vemos. See you. (informal) Chao. Bye. (informal, common in Latin America) Dialogue At a language exchange, two people meet. Marta: ¡Hola! Me llamo Marta. ¿Y tú? (Hi! My name is Marta. And you?) Tom: Hola, Marta. Soy Tom. (Hi, Marta. I'm Tom.) Marta: Mucho gusto, Tom. (Nice to meet you, Tom.) Tom: Igualmente. ¿Qué tal? (Likewise. How's it going?) Marta: Muy bien, gracias. Mira, esta es Elena. (Very well, thanks. Look, this is Elena.) Elena: Hola, Tom. Encantada. (Hi, Tom. Pleased to meet you.) Tom: Encantado, Elena. (Pleased to meet you, Elena.) Marta: Bueno, hasta luego. (OK, see you later.) Tom and Elena: ¡Hasta luego! (See you later!) Culture note In Spain friends and new acquaintances often greet with two kisses, one on each cheek; in much of Latin America one kiss is usual. Men usually shake hands with each other. Buenas on its own is a friendly short form of buenos días or buenas tardes. When in doubt, start with usted. People will tell you if they prefer tú: Puedes tutearme (\"you can say tú to me\"). Common mistakes ✗ Buenos tardes. → ✓ Buenas tardes. — tarde and noche are feminine words, so it is buenas; only días takes buenos. ✗ Me llamo es Ana. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. — me llamo already means \"my name is\"; there is no extra verb. ✗ Encantado (said by a woman) → ✓ Encantada — the word agrees with the person speaking. ✗ ¿Cómo te llamas? (to an older stranger at an office) → ✓ ¿Cómo se llama usted? — use the formal version with people you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". ✗ Bien, y tú? → ✓ Bien, ¿y tú? — a question inside a sentence still opens with ¿. Practice Choose the right greeting. It is 9 a.m. You walk into a bakery: (Buenos días / Buenas noches) It is 4 p.m.: (Buenas tardes / Buenos tardes) Complete the dialogue. —Hola, me Carlos. —Mucho gusto. Yo soy Rosa. —Encantada. —. (Likewise.) —¿Qué tal? —Bien, gracias. ¿Y ? (to a friend) Translate into Spanish. What's your name? (to your teacher's grandmother) See you tomorrow! Pleased to meet you. (said by a woman) Answers Buenos días Buenas tardes llamo Igualmente tú ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama? ¡Hasta mañana! Encantada. / Mucho gusto.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns","group":"pages","title":"Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"I, you, he, she, we, they in Spanish; four words for \"you\"; and why Spanish often leaves the pronoun out.","text":"Subject pronouns are the words for \"I\", \"you\", \"she\" and so on. Spanish has more of them than English, for two reasons. First, \"you\" has a friendly form and a polite form, and a singular and a plural. Second, \"we\" and \"they\" show whether the group is all female. But Spanish uses them much less, because the verb ending already shows who is meant. How it works English Spanish Notes I yo no capital letter you (one friend) tú with an accent; tu without it means \"your\" he / she él / ella él with an accent; el without it means \"the\" you (one person, formal) usted often written Ud. we nosotros / nosotras nosotras: only women you (a group of friends) (Spain) vosotros / vosotras vosotras: only women they ellos / ellas ellas: only women you (a group) ustedes formal in Spain; all groups in Latin America. Often Uds. A mixed group takes the masculine form: Ana and Pedro are ellos; Ana, Rosa and Pedro are still ellos. Usted and ustedes mean \"you\", but they take the same verb form as él and ellos: ¿Cómo está usted? is \"How are you?\" There is no word for \"it\" as a subject: Es grande. (It is big.) When to use it Choose tú or usted. Tú for friends, family, children and people your age in relaxed settings. Usted for older people, customers, officials and anyone you would call \"sir\" or \"madam\". To a friend: ¿Y tú? To your new boss: ¿Y usted? Talk to a group. In Spain, vosotros for friends and ustedes for formal groups. In Latin America, ustedes for every group. Both are correct; use the one people use where you are. Leave the pronoun out. In a normal sentence the verb is enough: Me llamo Ana (not Yo me llamo Ana every time). Use the pronoun for contrast or emphasis. Yo me llamo Ana y él se llama Pedro. (I'm Ana, and he is Pedro.) —¿Quién es Marta? —Yo. (Me.) Examples Spanish English —Bien, gracias. ¿Y tú? Fine, thanks. And you? —Muy bien. ¿Y usted, señora? Very well. And you, madam? Él se llama Luis y ella se llama Rosa. He's called Luis and she's called Rosa. ¿Cómo se llama usted? What's your name? (formal) —¿Y vosotros? —Nosotros, bien. (Spain) \"And you guys?\" \"We're fine.\" —¿Y ustedes? —Nosotras, muy bien. \"And you?\" \"We're very well.\" (a group of women) Mucho gusto. Yo soy Pablo. Nice to meet you. I'm Pablo. Good to know In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America (and in parts of other countries) people say vos instead of tú with different verb forms (vos sos, vos tenés). You will understand it from context; this course uses tú. Common mistakes ✗ Yo me llamo Ana. Yo soy de Lima. Yo soy profesora. → ✓ Me llamo Ana. Soy de Lima. Soy profesora. — repeating yo in every sentence sounds unnatural. ✗ Usted te llamas Pedro. → ✓ Usted se llama Pedro. — usted takes the forms of él, not of tú. ✗ Ellos (for Ana and María) → ✓ Ellas — a group of only women is ellas, and nosotras for \"we\". ✗ Él es grande. (about a table) → ✓ Es grande. — there is no subject pronoun for \"it\". ✗ tu (for \"you\") → ✓ tú — without the accent, tu means \"your\". Practice Which pronoun? Write it in Spanish. you, to your best friend you, to an old man you meet in a museum they (Elena and Sofía) we (you and your brother; you are a woman) Translate into Spanish. And you? (to a group of friends in Mexico) And you? (to a group of friends in Madrid) He's called Pablo. What's your name? (to a hotel receptionist) Answers tú usted ellas nosotros ¿Y ustedes? ¿Y vosotros? / ¿Y vosotras? (¿Y ustedes? is also understood) Se llama Pablo. / Él se llama Pablo. ¿Cómo se llama usted? / ¿Cómo se llama?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-subject-pronouns"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-ser","group":"pages","title":"ser: identity, origin and profession","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The first \"to be\": say who you are, where you are from, what you do and what someone is like.","text":"English has one verb \"to be\". Spanish has two, ser and estar, and they are not interchangeable. This page is about ser, the verb for what something is: a name, a nationality, a job, a lasting quality. You will meet estar (where something is, how someone feels) in unit 3. Ser is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. How it works Person ser English yo soy I am tú eres you are él, ella, usted es he / she is, you are (formal) nosotros, nosotras somos we are vosotros, vosotras (Spain) sois you are (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes son they are, you are (plural) Negative: put no before the verb: No soy de aquí. (I'm not from here.) Question: the same words, with ¿ ? and rising intonation: ¿Eres de México? (Are you from Mexico?) Origin: ser + de + place: Soy de Chile. Jobs: no un / una before the job: Es médico. (He is a doctor.) When to use it Name and identity. Soy Marta. (I'm Marta.) Es mi amigo. (He is my friend.) Origin and nationality. Somos de Colombia. (We're from Colombia.) Es español. (He is Spanish.) Nationalities are written with a small letter. Job. Soy profesor. (I'm a teacher; a woman says profesora.) Son estudiantes. (They are students.) What someone or something is like (a lasting quality). Es inteligente. (She is smart.) Eres muy amable. (You are very kind.) Day and date. Hoy es lunes. (Today is Monday.) Examples Spanish English Soy Daniel. Soy de Canadá. I'm Daniel. I'm from Canada. ¿Eres estudiante? Are you a student? Mi madre es médica. My mother is a doctor. No somos de aquí. We're not from here. ¿De dónde es usted? Where are you from? (formal) Estos son Ana y Luis. Son de Madrid. This is Ana and Luis. They're from Madrid. Mi profesora es muy simpática. My teacher is very nice. ¿Sois hermanos? (Spain) Are you brothers? / Are you brother and sister? Good to know Es can mean \"he is\", \"she is\", \"it is\" and \"you are\" (formal). The context tells you which; add él, ella or usted only if it is not clear. With a job plus a description, the article comes back: Es un médico muy bueno. (He is a very good doctor.) For age Spanish uses tener, not ser: Tengo 25 años. See . Common mistakes ✗ Soy un profesor. → ✓ Soy profesor. — no un / una before a job on its own. ✗ Soy 20 años. → ✓ Tengo 20 años. — age is \"having years\" in Spanish. ✗ Yo es de Chile. → ✓ Yo soy de Chile. — each person has its own form: yo soy, él es. ✗ Es en Madrid. (where a person is) → ✓ Está en Madrid. — location of people and things takes estar; ser de is for origin (events are different; you will see them later). Practice Complete with the right form of ser. Yo de Perú. ¿Tú profesora? Nosotros amigos. Ellas de Italia. Usted muy amable. Translate into Spanish. I'm not from here. She is a nurse. Where are you from? (to a friend) Answers soy eres somos son es No soy de aquí. Es enfermera. / Ella es enfermera. ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde eres tú?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-ser"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count to one hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\" the Spanish way.","text":"Numbers, days and months are everywhere: prices, phone numbers, plans. Two things surprise English speakers. Days and months are written with a small letter in Spanish. And you say \"on Monday\" without any word for \"on\": just el lunes. Words Numbers 0–20 Spanish English cero, uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 seis, siete, ocho, nueve, diez 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 once, doce, trece, catorce, quince 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 dieciséis, diecisiete, dieciocho, diecinueve, veinte 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 Numbers 21–100 Spanish English veintiuno, veintidós, veintitrés 21, 22, 23 veinticuatro … veintinueve 24 … 29 treinta, treinta y uno, treinta y dos 30, 31, 32 cuarenta, cincuenta, sesenta 40, 50, 60 setenta, ochenta, noventa 70, 80, 90 noventa y nueve 99 cien 100 16–29 are one word: diecisiete, veintiocho. From 31 they are three words with y: cuarenta y cinco. Watch the accents: dieciséis, veintidós, veintitrés, veintiséis. Uno becomes un before a masculine noun and una before a feminine one: un euro, una persona, veintiún años. Days of the week (the week starts on Monday) Spanish English el lunes Monday el martes Tuesday el miércoles Wednesday el jueves Thursday el viernes Friday el sábado Saturday el domingo Sunday el fin de semana the weekend Months Spanish English enero, febrero, marzo January, February, March abril, mayo, junio April, May, June julio, agosto, septiembre July, August, September octubre, noviembre, diciembre October, November, December Word partners Spanish English Hoy es martes. Today is Tuesday. el lunes on Monday (this coming Monday) los lunes on Mondays (every Monday) de lunes a viernes from Monday to Friday el fin de semana on the weekend en julio in July en verano in summer el número de teléfono the phone number Mi número es seis, cinco, cero… My number is six, five, zero… ¿Cuánto es? —Son veinte euros. How much is it? —It's twenty euros. Good to know Phone numbers are read digit by digit or in pairs: 61 20 34 → sesenta y uno, veinte, treinta y cuatro. Septiembre is also spelled setiembre in some countries. Days ending in -s do not change in the plural: el lunes, los lunes. Sábado and domingo add -s: los sábados. Common mistakes ✗ Hoy es Lunes. → ✓ Hoy es lunes. — days and months take a small letter. ✗ en el lunes → ✓ el lunes — Spanish has no word for \"on\" with days. ✗ veinte y dos → ✓ veintidós — today 21–29 are written as one word. ✗ en Julio → ✓ en julio — months also take a small letter. Practice Write the number in words. 15 22 48 100 Translate into Spanish. on Saturday on Fridays in October Today is Wednesday. Answers quince veintidós cuarenta y ocho cien el sábado los viernes en octubre Hoy es miércoles.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-gender-number","group":"pages","title":"Gender and number of nouns","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine. Learn the endings that tell you which, and how to make plurals.","text":"In English a table is just \"it\". In Spanish every noun is masculine or feminine, even a table or an idea: el libro (the book) is masculine, la mesa (the table) is feminine. The gender has nothing to do with the thing itself, but it matters because the words around the noun change with it. Learn every new noun together with el or la. How it works Gender: most nouns show it in the ending. Ending Usually Examples -o masculine el libro, el vaso, el perro -a feminine la mesa, la casa, la playa -ción, -sión, -dad, -tad feminine la canción, la televisión, la ciudad, la libertad -or, -aje masculine el color, el viaje -e or a consonant (other than -or, -aje) either: learn it el coche, la noche; el hotel, la piel Common exceptions: el día, el mapa, el problema, el idioma, el sofá; la mano, la foto, la moto, la radio (el radio for the device in Mexico and parts of Latin America), la flor. People: many nouns change the ending (el amigo, la amiga; el profesor, la profesora). Nouns in -ista and -e often keep one form: el / la estudiante, el / la periodista. Number: making the plural. Singular ends in Add Examples a vowel -s libro → libros, coche → coches a consonant -es hotel → hoteles, ciudad → ciudades -z z → -ces lápiz → lápices, vez → veces el → los, la → las: los libros, las mesas. When a word stressed on the last syllable ends in -n or -s and adds -es, the written accent disappears: canción → canciones, inglés → ingleses. A stressed final vowel keeps it: sofá → sofás. Words in unstressed -s do not change: el lunes → los lunes. A group of men and women takes the masculine plural: los alumnos can be all boys or boys and girls. When to use it Every time you learn a noun. Learn la ciudad, not just ciudad. The article is your note of the gender. When you meet a new noun without an article. Look at the ending and guess with the table; you will be right most of the time. Talking about more than one. Dos hoteles, tres canciones, veinte días. Talking about mixed groups. Mis hermanos (mis = my) can mean \"my brothers\" or \"my brothers and sisters\". Examples Spanish English el día y la noche the day and the night la mano the hand el problema the problem las ciudades the cities los lápices the pencils dos canciones two songs los estudiantes the students (all men, or men and women) las estudiantes the students (all women) Good to know Some nouns change meaning with the gender: el capital (money) and la capital (the capital city). Most countries and cities are used without an article: Soy de Perú. Son de Madrid. Spanish has no neuter \"it\" nouns: everything is el or la. Common mistakes ✗ la problema → ✓ el problema — problema ends in -a but is masculine. ✗ el mano → ✓ la mano — mano ends in -o but is feminine. ✗ las ciudads → ✓ las ciudades — after a consonant the plural adds -es. ✗ los lápizs → ✓ los lápices — a final z becomes c before -es. Practice El or la? libro canción día foto Write the plural with the article. el hotel la ciudad el lápiz la canción Answers el la el la los hoteles las ciudades los lápices las canciones","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-gender-number"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-articles","group":"pages","title":"Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"el, la, los, las and un, una, unos, unas; when Spanish uses an article where English does not, and a + el = al, de + el = del.","text":"Spanish articles work like English \"the\" and \"a\", with two differences. They change with the gender and number of the noun, so there are four of each. And Spanish uses \"the\" more often than English: for things in general (la vida, life), with titles (el señor García) and with days (el lunes). If your language has no articles at all, this is the topic to practice most. How it works masculine feminine the (singular) el libro la mesa the (plural) los libros las mesas a / an un libro una mesa some unos libros unas mesas Two short forms. A (to) and de (of, from) join with el: a + el = al: el cine → Voy al cine. (I'm going to the movies.) de + el = del: el profesor → el libro del profesor (the teacher's book) Only with el: a la, de la, a los, de las do not change. A feminine noun that starts with a stressed a- takes el and un in the singular, to avoid two a sounds: el agua (water), un aula (a classroom). It is still feminine: el agua fría, las aguas. When to use it Something specific, or already mentioned: el, la, los, las. Es la casa de Ana. (It's Ana's house.) Something new, one of many: un, una. Es un problema. (It's a problem.) Things in general take el / la / los / las, where English uses no article: El café es caro aquí. (Coffee is expensive here.) Los niños son curiosos. (Children are curious.) Talking about a person with a title: el señor García, la doctora Ruiz. But not when you speak to the person: Buenos días, señor García. Days of the week: el lunes (on Monday), los lunes (on Mondays). No article with a job after ser (Es médico), with most countries (Soy de Perú), and for an unspecified amount of something: Hay pan y leche. (There's bread and milk.) Examples Spanish English Es un libro. Es el libro de Marta. It's a book. It's Marta's book. Las mesas son nuevas. The tables are new. El café es caro aquí. Coffee is expensive here. El señor García es de Chile. Mr. García is from Chile. Buenos días, señora Ruiz. Good morning, Mrs. Ruiz. Voy al cine el sábado. I'm going to the movies on Saturday. Es el coche del profesor. It's the teacher's car. Son unos amigos de México. They're friends from Mexico. Good to know Voy (I'm going) is a form of ir; you will learn it later. Here it only shows al. De + person shows possession, where English has 's: la casa de Ana (Ana's house). Common mistakes ✗ Coche es rojo. → ✓ El coche es rojo. — a singular noun as the subject needs an article. ✗ Voy a el cine. → ✓ Voy al cine. — a + el always joins into al. ✗ el libro de el profesor → ✓ el libro del profesor — de + el joins into del. ✗ El señor García, ¿cómo está? (speaking to him) → ✓ Señor García, ¿cómo está? — no article when you speak directly to someone. ✗ la agua → ✓ el agua — a feminine noun with a stressed a- takes el in the singular. Practice Write el, la, los, las, un or una. Es casa muy bonita. (a) niños son simpáticos. (the) ciudad es grande. (the) Hay problema. (a) Write al, del or de la. Voy cine. (el cine) Es el perro vecino. (el vecino) Es la casa profesora. (la profesora) Translate: Good afternoon, Mr. López. Answers una Los La un al del de la Buenas tardes, señor López.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-articles"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement","group":"pages","title":"Adjective agreement and position","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Spanish adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number, and they usually come after it: una casa blanca.","text":"An English adjective never changes: a white house, two white cars. A Spanish adjective follows its noun in two ways. It agrees: it takes the same gender and number as the noun. And it usually comes after the noun: una casa blanca is word for word \"a house white\". How it works Agreement. Adjectives have up to four forms. Adjective ends in masculine feminine masculine plural feminine plural -o alto alta altos altas -e grande grande grandes grandes a consonant azul azul azules azules nationality in a consonant español española españoles españolas -e and most consonant adjectives have one form for both genders: un coche grande, una casa grande; un libro fácil, una pregunta fácil. Nationalities in a consonant add -a for the feminine: inglés → inglesa, alemán → alemana. The accent disappears when a syllable is added. A mixed group takes the masculine plural: Juan y Ana son altos. Position. Most adjectives go after the noun: un coche rojo, una ciudad bonita, unos chicos simpáticos. These go before the noun: numbers and quantities: dos libros, mucho tiempo, pocas personas; bueno and malo often do, and they lose the -o before a masculine singular noun: un buen amigo, un mal día; grande before a singular noun becomes gran and means \"great, important\": un gran hombre (a great man), un hombre grande (a big man). When to use it Describing things. Es un coche rojo. (It's a red car.) Describing people with ser. Mi hermana es alta. Mis amigos son simpáticos. Saying where someone is from. Es mexicana. (She is Mexican.) Son españoles. (They are Spanish.) Nationalities take a small letter. Giving an opinion with muy. La casa es muy bonita. (The house is very pretty.) Muy never changes. Examples Spanish English una casa blanca a white house unos chicos simpáticos some nice boys (or boys and girls) Las calles son muy estrechas. The streets are very narrow. Mi profesora es colombiana. My teacher is Colombian. Es un libro fácil. It's an easy book. Los zapatos son azules. The shoes are blue. Es un buen restaurante. It's a good restaurant. Es un gran hombre. He's a great man. Good to know An adjective can also stand after ser, far from the noun, and it still agrees: La sopa es deliciosa. Los tomates son caros. Colors that are also nouns, like naranja (orange) and rosa (pink), usually do not change: unas flores naranja. Both flores naranja and flores naranjas are heard. Common mistakes ✗ la casa blanco → ✓ la casa blanca — the adjective takes the gender of the noun. ✗ un rojo coche → ✓ un coche rojo — colors and most adjectives come after the noun. ✗ las chicas simpáticos → ✓ las chicas simpáticas — gender and number both agree. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — nationalities in a consonant have a feminine form. ✗ un bueno amigo → ✓ un buen amigo — bueno loses the -o before a masculine singular noun. Practice Write the right form of the adjective. una ciudad (bonito) unos coches (rojo) unas mesas (grande) María es (español) Put the words in order. coche / un / azul amigo / un / buen simpáticas / las / chicas / son Translate: It's a white house. Answers una ciudad bonita unos coches rojos unas mesas grandes María es española. un coche azul un buen amigo Las chicas son simpáticas. Es una casa blanca.","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-adjective-agreement"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-tener","group":"pages","title":"tener: possession, age and common expressions","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Tener means \"to have\". Spanish also uses it for age and for hunger, thirst, heat, cold and fear.","text":"Tener means \"to have\": Tengo un perro. (I have a dog.) Spanish uses it in more places than English does. Your age is something you \"have\": Tengo 30 años. And many feelings of the body are things you \"have\" too: Tengo hambre (I'm hungry, word for word \"I have hunger\"). Where English says \"I am…\", Spanish often says tengo. How it works Person tener English yo tengo I have tú tienes you have él, ella, usted tiene he / she has, you have (formal) nosotros, nosotras tenemos we have vosotros, vosotras (Spain) tenéis you have (plural) ellos, ellas, ustedes tienen they have, you have (plural) The yo form ends in -go: tengo. The stem changes to tie- in tienes, tiene and tienen, but not in tengo, tenemos and tenéis. Negative: No tengo hermanos. After no, a single countable thing often has no article: No tengo coche. (more natural than No tengo un coche) Question: ¿Tienes hermanos? (Do you have any brothers or sisters?) Expressions with tener (the noun after tener does not change): Spanish English tener hambre to be hungry tener sed to be thirsty tener calor / frío to be hot / cold (a person) tener sueño to be sleepy tener miedo to be afraid tener prisa to be in a hurry tener razón to be right To say \"very\", use mucho or mucha with the gender of the noun: mucho frío, mucho calor, but mucha hambre, mucha sed, mucha prisa. When to use it Possession. Tengo un coche nuevo. (I have a new car.) Family and friends. Tenemos dos hijos. (We have two children.) ¿Tienes novio? (Do you have a boyfriend?) Age. ¿Cuántos años tienes? (How old are you?) Tengo veintiún años. (I'm twenty-one.) How your body feels. Tengo frío. (I'm cold.) ¿Tienes hambre? (Are you hungry?) Agreeing with someone. Tienes razón. (You're right.) Examples Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos y una hermana. I have two brothers and a sister. ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? How old are you? (formal) Mi abuela tiene ochenta años. My grandmother is eighty. No tenemos coche. We don't have a car. ¿Tenéis hambre? (Spain) Are you (all) hungry? Los niños tienen sueño. The children are sleepy. Tengo mucho calor. I'm very hot. Tienes razón. You're right. Good to know Tengo calor is about how you feel. For the weather, Spanish says Hace calor (it's hot); you will learn it later. Estar caliente about a person can have a sexual meaning. Say tengo calor. Tener que + infinitive means \"to have to\": Tengo que trabajar. It comes in A2. Common mistakes ✗ Soy 25 años. → ✓ Tengo 25 años. — age takes tener, not ser. ✗ Estoy caliente. (I feel hot) → ✓ Tengo calor. — the body feeling is tener calor. ✗ Tengo mucho hambre. → ✓ Tengo mucha hambre. — hambre is feminine (el hambre, like el agua). ✗ Estoy hambre. → ✓ Tengo hambre. — hambre is a noun (hunger), so you \"have\" it. Practice Complete with the right form of tener. Yo un gato. ¿Cuántos años (tú)? Nosotros dos hijas. Ellos no coche. Translate into Spanish. I'm hungry. She is twenty years old. Are you cold? (to a friend) You're right. (formal) Answers tengo tienes tenemos tienen Tengo hambre. Tiene veinte años. / Ella tiene veinte años. ¿Tienes frío? Usted tiene razón. / Tiene razón.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-tener"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-personal-details","group":"pages","title":"Give and ask for personal details","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Ask and answer where you are from, how old you are, what you do, your phone number and e-mail, in both tú and usted.","text":"Forms, language courses, hotels and new friends all ask the same few questions. With ser and tener you can already answer most of them. Two things are different from English: \"What is your name / number?\" is ¿Cuál es…?, not ¿Qué es…?, and \"How old are you?\" uses tener: ¿Cuántos años tienes? Learn each question in two versions: with tú for people your age and with usted at an office or a reception desk. Key phrases Asking (tú / usted) Spanish English ¿Cómo te llamas? / ¿Cómo se llama? What's your name? ¿De dónde eres? / ¿De dónde es? Where are you from? ¿Cuántos años tienes? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? How old are you? ¿A qué te dedicas? / ¿A qué se dedica? What do you do (for a living)? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? / ¿Cuál es su número? What's your phone number? ¿Cuál es tu correo electrónico? / ¿Cuál es su correo? What's your e-mail address? ¿Cómo se escribe? How do you spell it? Answering Spanish English Me llamo Ana García. My name is Ana García. Soy de Canadá. Soy canadiense. I'm from Canada. I'm Canadian. Tengo veintiocho años. I'm twenty-eight. Soy enfermera. / Soy estudiante. I'm a nurse. / I'm a student. Mi número es el 6 47 31 25 08. My number is 6 47 31 25 08. Es ana punto garcía arroba correo punto com. It's ana.garcia@correo.com. Con tilde / sin tilde. With / without an accent. Checking Spanish English ¿Perdón? ¿Puedes repetir? / ¿Puede repetir? Sorry? Can you repeat that? ¿Y tú? / ¿Y usted? And you? Dialogue At the reception desk of a language school. Receptionist: Buenos días. ¿Cómo se llama? (Good morning. What's your name?) Daniel: Daniel Wright. (Daniel Wright.) Receptionist: ¿Cómo se escribe Wright? (How do you spell Wright?) Daniel: Uve doble, erre, i, ge, hache, te. (W, R, I, G, H, T.) Receptionist: Gracias. ¿De dónde es? (Thank you. Where are you from?) Daniel: Soy de Toronto. Soy canadiense. (I'm from Toronto. I'm Canadian.) Receptionist: ¿Y cuántos años tiene? (And how old are you?) Daniel: Treinta y cuatro. (Thirty-four.) Receptionist: ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? (What's your phone number?) Daniel: Es el seis, cuarenta y siete, treinta y uno, veinticinco, cero ocho. (It's 6 47 31 25 08.) Culture note In Spain phone numbers are often read in pairs or threes; in many Latin American countries digit by digit is more common. Both are understood. Spanish speakers have two family names, the father's and the mother's: Ana García López. On forms, apellidos means \"surnames\". @ is arroba, . is punto, - is guion and is guion bajo. W is uve doble (Spain), doble ve or doble u (Latin America). Common mistakes ✗ ¿Qué es tu número? → ✓ ¿Cuál es tu número? — \"what is\" + a piece of information is ¿cuál es…? ✗ ¿Cuántos años eres? → ✓ ¿Cuántos años tienes? — age takes tener. ✗ Soy Argentino. → ✓ Soy argentino. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Soy un estudiante. → ✓ Soy estudiante. — no article before a job or role on its own. Practice Write the question for each answer (use tú). —Me llamo Sofía. —Soy de Chile. —Tengo diecinueve años. —Soy camarero. Write the formal (usted) version. ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿Cuál es tu número de teléfono? Translate: I'm a student and I'm twenty years old. Answers ¿Cómo te llamas? ¿De dónde eres? ¿Cuántos años tienes? ¿A qué te dedicas? ¿De dónde es usted? / ¿De dónde es? ¿Cuántos años tiene usted? / ¿Cuántos años tiene? ¿Cuál es su número de teléfono? Soy estudiante y tengo veinte años.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-personal-details"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-family-people","group":"pages","title":"Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Words for your family, how people look and what they are like, where they are from and what they do.","text":"With ser and tener you can now talk about the people in your life: Tengo una hermana. Es alta y muy simpática. Es médica. Remember three rules from earlier pages: adjectives and many nouns change for women (el médico, la médica), nationalities take a small letter, and there is no un / una before a job on its own. Words Family Spanish English el padre / la madre father / mother los padres parents (or fathers) el hermano / la hermana brother / sister los hermanos brothers, or brothers and sisters el hijo / la hija son / daughter los hijos children (sons and daughters) el abuelo / la abuela grandfather / grandmother el tío / la tía uncle / aunt el primo / la prima cousin (male / female) el marido / la mujer husband / wife el novio / la novia boyfriend / girlfriend Describing people Spanish English alto / bajo tall / short joven / mayor young / older, elderly rubio / moreno blond / dark-haired delgado slim simpático / antipático nice / unfriendly inteligente smart divertido fun tímido shy Nationalities (small letter) Spanish English español / española Spanish mexicano / mexicana Mexican colombiano / colombiana Colombian argentino / argentina Argentinian estadounidense American (from the US) canadiense Canadian inglés / inglesa English alemán / alemana German Jobs Spanish English el médico / la médica doctor el enfermero / la enfermera nurse el profesor / la profesora teacher el / la estudiante student el camarero / la camarera waiter / waitress (Spain; mesero in Mexico, mozo in Argentina) el ingeniero / la ingeniera engineer el / la policía police officer (la policía also means \"the police\") el abogado / la abogada lawyer Word partners Spanish English Tengo dos hermanos. I have two brothers (or a brother and a sister). Es hijo único. He's an only child. Mi hermana es alta y morena. My sister is tall and dark-haired. Mis padres son muy simpáticos. My parents are very nice. Mi abuelo es mayor y tiene noventa años. My grandfather is old; he's ninety. Es de Colombia, es colombiana. She's from Colombia; she's Colombian. Mi madre es profesora. My mother is a teacher. ¿Tienes novio? Do you have a boyfriend? Es muy divertido. He's a lot of fun. Good to know El marido / la mujer are used everywhere; el esposo / la esposa are also common, especially in Latin America. Mayor is the polite word for an old person. Viejo can sound rude about people. Americano can mean anyone from the Americas; estadounidense is only \"from the US\". Common mistakes ✗ mis parientes (meaning \"my parents\") → ✓ mis padres — parientes means \"relatives\". ✗ Es Mexicana. → ✓ Es mexicana. — nationalities take a small letter. ✗ Mi hermana es alto. → ✓ Mi hermana es alta. — the adjective agrees with hermana. ✗ Ella es inglés. → ✓ Ella es inglesa. — the feminine adds -a. Practice Write the female form. el abuelo el profesor Es español. Es alemán. Translate into Spanish. my parents I have a sister. He's tall and nice. She's a nurse. Answers la abuela la profesora Es española. Es alemana. mis padres Tengo una hermana. Es alto y simpático. Es enfermera.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-family-people"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-gustar","group":"pages","title":"gustar and similar verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.","text":"In English you say \"I like coffee\", and I is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: Me gusta el café is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: gusta and gustan. How it works (a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like Who likes it Pronoun One thing, or an action Several things (a mí) me gusta gustan (a ti) te gusta gustan (a él, a ella, a usted) le gusta gustan (a nosotros, a nosotras) nos gusta gustan (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) os gusta gustan (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) les gusta gustan gusta + singular noun with its article: Me gusta el café. gusta + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar. gustan + plural noun: Me gustan los gatos. Negative: no goes before the pronoun: No me gusta el té. Questions keep the same word order: ¿Te gusta el fútbol? When to use it Say what you like or don't like. Me gusta la música. (I like music.) No me gustan los lunes. (I don't like Mondays.) Ask someone. To a friend: ¿Te gusta cocinar? Formal: ¿Le gusta la habitación? Say how much. Me gusta mucho el cine. (I like movies a lot.) No me gusta nada el frío. (I don't like the cold at all.) Me encanta means \"I love\": Me encanta esta ciudad. Make clear who. Le can mean he, she or you (formal), so add a + the person: A María le gusta el cine. A mí no. Agree or disagree. —Me gusta el café. —A mí también. (Me too.) —No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco. (Me neither.) Examples Spanish English Me gusta el chocolate. I like chocolate. ¿Te gustan los perros? Do you like dogs? No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. We don't like working on Saturdays. A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. Carlos really likes traveling. A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. My parents like old movies. Me encanta esta ciudad. I love this city. ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) Do you all like Mexican food? —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" Good to know The same pattern works for encantar (to love), interesar (to interest) and doler (to hurt): Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza. Me encanta is already strong. Do not add mucho. About people, Me gustas means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use Me cae bien: Tu hermano me cae muy bien. Common mistakes ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — you are not the subject; the coffee is. ✗ Me gusta los gatos. → ✓ Me gustan los gatos. — the verb agrees with the thing you like. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — things you like in general need the article. ✗ María le gusta bailar. → ✓ A María le gusta bailar. — the person needs a. ✗ Me gusto bailar. → ✓ Me gusta bailar. — me gusto means \"I like myself\". Practice Complete with the pronoun and gusta or gustan. (a mí) la paella. (a ti) ¿ los deportes? A Ana leer. (a nosotros) No las verduras. A mis amigos cantar y bailar. Translate into Spanish. I don't like coffee. Do you like the hotel? (formal) \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\" Answers Me gusta Te gustan le gusta nos gustan les gusta No me gusta el café. ¿Le gusta el hotel? —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-gustar"},{"id":"review","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: deck cards, My mistakes and weak spots from exercises.","text":"flashcards review decks mistakes weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"review:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with their sentence and meaning.","text":"my words personal vocabulary flashcards","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-food-shopping","group":"exercises","title":"Food, drink and shopping (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 8 cards. The full deck has 30–40 cards for the topic a1-vocabulary-food-shopping.","text":"flashcards deck Food, drink and shopping A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-food-shopping"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-1","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, build sentences and translate.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-2","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: write it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the missing word, rewrite sentences, write what you hear and build a question.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-2"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-3","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: agree, disagree and say how much","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: listen and write, rewrite, find the mistake, match reactions and fill a table with encantar.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and spelling: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: stress, special letters, accents and question marks.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1","group":"exercises","title":"Subject pronouns: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the right pronoun, tú or usted, and when to leave the pronoun out.","text":"Topic: Subject pronouns; tú vs usted","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-subject-pronouns-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-ser-1","group":"exercises","title":"ser: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of ser, origin, jobs and descriptions.","text":"Topic: ser: identity, origin and profession","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-ser-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-gender-number-1","group":"exercises","title":"Gender and plurals: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el or la, plural endings and the usual exceptions.","text":"Topic: Gender and number of nouns","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-gender-number-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-articles-1","group":"exercises","title":"Articles: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: el, la, un, una, al and del, and when to use no article.","text":"Topic: Definite and indefinite articles; al and del","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-articles-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1","group":"exercises","title":"Adjectives: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: agreement, position, nationalities and buen / gran.","text":"Topic: Adjective agreement and position","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-adjective-agreement-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-tener-1","group":"exercises","title":"tener: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: the forms of tener, age, family and tener hambre / frío.","text":"Topic: tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-tener-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and spelling, subject pronouns and ser, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, pronunciation and stress, Subject pronouns; tú vs usted, ser: identity, origin and profession","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u2","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: People and things","headings":[],"summary":"Gender and plurals, articles, adjectives and tener, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Gender and number of nouns, Definite and indefinite articles; al and del, Adjective agreement and position, tener: possession, age and common expressions","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u2"},{"id":"lab:presente","group":"lab","title":"Presente","headings":["Present"],"summary":"What is true now, what you do regularly, and general facts.","text":"True now Vivo en un apartamento pequeño cerca del centro. I live in a small apartment near downtown. Habits and routines Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermana. On Saturdays I play tennis with my sister. General facts El sol sale por el este. The sun rises in the east. Happening at the moment ¿Qué haces? —Leo una novela. What are you doing? —I'm reading a novel. Fixed plans and timetables Mañana trabajo hasta las ocho. Tomorrow I'm working until eight. siempre normalmente todos los días los lunes a veces nunca ahora English says “I'm reading” for an action going on now. Spanish can just use the present: Leo una novela. Estoy leyendo is also very common, but you do not need it every time. The ending already shows the person, so the subject pronoun is usually left out: Vivo en Lima. Using yo in every sentence sounds unnatural; keep it for emphasis or contrast. Stem changes do not happen in the nosotros and vosotros forms: queremos, podemos, not *quieremos, *puedemos.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/presente"},{"id":"lab:preterito-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito indefinido","headings":["Preterite"],"summary":"Finished actions at a definite time in the past.","text":"One finished action Ayer comí con Lucía. Yesterday I had lunch with Lucía. A chain of actions Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. I got home, had dinner and went to bed. A closed period of time Viví tres años en Lima. I lived in Lima for three years. ayer anoche el lunes la semana pasada en 2019 hace dos años The English simple past covers both one finished event and a habit: “I ate there yesterday” / “As a kid I ate there every Sunday”. Spanish separates them: comí (once, finished) and comía (habit or background). Present yo and preterite él differ only by the accent: hablo (I speak) / habló (he or she spoke). The preterite yo form is hablé.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:preterito-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito imperfecto","headings":["Imperfect"],"summary":"Descriptions, background and habits in the past.","text":"Habits in the past De niña vivía en un pueblo e iba a la escuela a pie. As a girl I lived in a village and walked to school. Descriptions La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín enorme. The house was old and had a huge garden. Background to an event Hacía frío y llovía cuando llegamos. It was cold and raining when we arrived. Time and how someone felt Eran las once y yo tenía mucho sueño. It was eleven o'clock and I was very sleepy. antes de niño / de niña siempre todos los veranos normalmente mientras cuando era joven English “used to play” and “was playing” are both often jugaba in Spanish. The imperfecto covers habits and actions in progress. The imperfecto does not tell you that an action finished. For one finished event at a definite time, use the indefinido: Ayer vi a Pablo. Ayer veía a Pablo sounds unfinished, as if the story goes on. Watch the accents: hablábamos (only the nosotros form of -ar verbs), but every -er and -ir form has í: comía, vivíamos.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:ir-a-infinitivo","group":"lab","title":"ir a + infinitivo","headings":["going to + infinitive"],"summary":"Plans, intentions and things that are clearly about to happen.","text":"Plans Este verano vamos a viajar a Perú. This summer we're going to travel to Peru. Intentions El próximo año voy a estudiar más. Next year I'm going to study more. About to happen (you can see it) ¡Cuidado, te vas a caer! Careful, you're going to fall! Let's… (vamos a) Bueno, vamos a empezar. OK, let's start. mañana esta noche este fin de semana el próximo año la semana que viene pronto Do not leave out a: Voy a comer, not *Voy comer. After a comes the infinitive, never a conjugated verb: Vamos a salir, not *Vamos a salimos. English “I'm going to the movies” has no second verb. In Spanish that is ir a + a place: Voy al cine.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/ir-a-infinitivo"},{"id":"lab:ser-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser vs estar","headings":["what someone or something is","where it is and how it is now"],"summary":"Two verbs for “to be”: ser says what something is, estar says where it is and how it is right now.","text":"Identity and jobs Who or what someone is, including their job. Origin Where someone or something comes from. What it is like Lasting characteristics: personality, looks, size, color. Events Where or when an event happens: a party, a class, a concert. Time and dates The time, the day and the date. Location Where a person or a thing is, even when it never moves. (Where an event happens takes ser.) Feelings and states How someone feels or what state something is in at the moment. bien and mal Asking and saying how someone is. *Soy cansado → Estoy cansado. Feelings and states take estar. *Está médico → Es médico. Jobs take ser. *La farmacia es en esta calle → La farmacia está en esta calle. Location takes estar. *Soy 20 años → Tengo 20 años. Age takes tener, not ser or estar.","level":"A1","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/ser-estar"},{"id":"lab-tree:ser-or-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser or estar?","headings":[],"summary":"Think of the sentence you want to say with “to be”. A few questions lead to the right verb, and sometimes it is neither of the two.","text":"What does “to be” tell us in your sentence? Where a person or a thing is estar Where an event happens: a party, a class, a concert ser Who someone is, their job, or where they are from ser The time, the day or the date ser How old someone is tener What someone or something is like, or how they are  Is it what they are like in general, or how they are at the moment? What they are like in general: personality, looks, size, color ser How they feel or how they are right now: tired, glad, ill, busy estar Just “How are you?” or “I'm fine” estar","level":"A1","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/ser-or-estar"}]};
